{"name":"Light Watermark","hex":"#b7dadd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"watermark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A translucent design impressed on the surface of paper and visible when the paper is held to the light.","A logo superimposed on a digital image, a television broadcast, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark paper with a watermark.","To mark a datafile with a digital watermark."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/watermark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Watermark","blocks":[{"text":"A watermark is an identifying image or pattern in paper that appears as varying shades of light and dark when viewed by either transmitted light or reflected light. These patterns are created from variations in the thickness or density of the paper. Watermarks have historically been used on postage stamps, currency, and other official documents to discourage counterfeiting. There are two primary methods of producing watermarks in paper: the dandy roll process and the more complex cylinder mould process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif/lossy-page1-330px-Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermark","wikidata":"Q43065"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Patentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Machine-made watermark on a 19th century letter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APatentpapierfabrik_zu_Penig%2C_Maschinen-Wasserzeichen_KRONENPOST_1898.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d4/Dandy_Roll.png/500px-Dandy_Roll.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d4/Dandy_Roll.png/1280px-Dandy_Roll.png?ut